    "phase": "preparation",
    "plan": {
      "G": "At closure, begin with the best individually measured candidate that passes the full correctness suite. Integrate only compatible measured changes, testing each combined program rather than assuming gains add. Confirm the final candidate independently and choose by the official geometric-mean kernel-time objective; if a combination fails correctness or confirmation, fall back to the best valid confirmed candidate. Preserve the kernel ABI, Particle layout, one-launch interface, and file restrictions.",
      "analysis": "Choose N=3: the baseline is a valid O(N²) one-thread-per-output kernel, with a reported mean of about 1.842 ms across three timing repeats. The supplied launch settings imply only 16, 32, and 64 CTAs at the three benchmark sizes, making inner-loop parallelism a distinct opportunity; its benefit is a hypothesis to measure, not an established bottleneck. The three objectives target separate axes: per-pair arithmetic, parallelism across each output's source sum, and CTA-level reuse of source data. The frozen target facts are a single GH200 and NVRTC CUDA 12.6.3; the baseline and target sizes are the only supplied performance evidence. I consulted the CUDA 12.6.3 Programming Guide's reciprocal-square-root discussion and its guidance on parallel execution, plus the general CUDA Best Practices Guide memory discussion: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/archive/12.6.3/cuda-c-programming-guide/index.html and https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-c-best-practices-guide/index.html. Compare candidates only after all outputs, edge cases, and numerical tolerances pass; judge performance by the official geometric mean and fresh confirmation.",
      "children": [
        {
          "objective": "In src/nbody_kernel.cu, investigate reducing pair-interaction math while retaining one thread per output. Try direct rsqrtf(distSq) with explicit reciprocal-cube multiplies and, if correct, algebraic cancellation of the target mass in the acceleration coefficient. Keep self-interaction skipping, float32 softening and update semantics, exact mass copying, and the existing single-kernel ABI. Do not enable global fast math or add launches or buffers. Validate every required case and compare the three-repeat benchmark geometric mean; provide the best correct source and launch.json.",
          "subject": "Pair-interaction arithmetic and reciprocal-square-root optimization"
        },
        {
          "objective": "Investigate assigning one warp to each output particle: lanes accumulate partial forces over distinct source indices and reduce the three components within the warp. Keep the pair arithmetic close to the supplied implementation to isolate this mapping change. Correctly handle tails, self-interaction, complete output state, and arbitrary supported N; tune block and items_per_block consistently. Validate the changed summation order against all required tolerances, then measure the official geometric mean and report the best correct source and launch.json.",
          "subject": "Warp-cooperative source summation per output"
        },
        {
          "objective": "Investigate staging source Particle records in CTA shared memory, with one thread owning each output and reusing each staged source across the CTA's outputs. Handle source and output tails, synchronization, and the allowed shared-memory limit; tune the tile and launch configuration without adding a kernel or allocation. Keep the supplied force arithmetic/order as far as practical to isolate the reuse strategy. Validate all required cases and compare the official geometric mean; provide the best correct source and launch.json.",
          "subject": "CTA shared-memory source tiling"
        }
      ]
    },
